{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T01:12:27Z","timestamp":1790125947661,"version":"4.0.1"},"reference-count":20,"publisher":"AIP Publishing","issue":"4","content-domain":{"domain":["pubs.aip.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":[],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1994,1,24]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The density of amorphous Si has been measured. Multiple Si implants, at energies up to 8.0 MeV, were made through a contact mask to produce alternating amorphous\/crystalline Si stripes with amorphous thicknesses up to \u223c5.0 \u03bcm. For layers up to 3.4 \u03bcm (5 MeV), the amorphous Si is constrained laterally and deforms plastically. Above 5 MeV, plastic deformation of the surrounding crystal matrix is observed. Height differences between the amorphous and crystalline regions were measured for as-implanted, thermally relaxed, and partially recrystallized samples using a surface profilometer. Combined with ion channeling measurements of the layer thickness, amorphous Si was determined to be 1.8\u00b10.1% less dense than crystalline Si (4.90\u00d71022 atom\/cm3 at 300 K). Both relaxed and unrelaxed amorphous Si show identical densities within experimental error (&amp;lt;0.1% density difference).<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/1.111121","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2002,7,26]],"date-time":"2002-07-26T08:38:20Z","timestamp":1027672700000},"page":"437-439","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/aip-crossmark-policy-page","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":275,"title":["Density of amorphous Si"],"prefix":"10.1063","volume":"64","author":[{"given":"J. S.","family":"Custer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Materials Science and Engineering, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York 14853"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Michael O.","family":"Thompson","sequence":"additi